WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:07.512
DAVID WHITE: Hi,
welcome to Google.

00:00:07.512 --> 00:00:08.470
My name is David White.

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:10.845
I'm going to talk to you today
about running your Windows

00:00:10.845 --> 00:00:13.530
workloads on Google Cloud.

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.230
I'm a Solution Manager here at
Google in the infrastructure

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:18.600
modernization group
and before joining

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.280
Google I've spent most of my
career as a COO, CIO, VP of IT.

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.830
Essentially I've spent more
of my time as the customer

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.362
than on this side of the table.

00:00:28.362 --> 00:00:30.570
So I want to talk to you a
little bit today about why

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:34.110
customers are running their
Microsoft workloads on GCP,

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:36.330
talk a little bit about
Microsoft licensing options

00:00:36.330 --> 00:00:39.210
in GCP, and then we'll talk
about phase transformation

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:43.140
of your Microsoft workloads in
your Microsoft applications.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:46.440
So the first question that we
often hear is, why on earth

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:50.220
would anybody bring Microsoft
workloads to Google Cloud?

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:53.970
And I think this more than
anything else answers it best.

00:00:53.970 --> 00:00:56.460
Urs Holzle, our
senior vice president

00:00:56.460 --> 00:00:58.860
of engineering and
technical infrastructure,

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:00.930
"We're committed to
making GCP a first class

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.720
platform for running
Microsoft workloads."

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:05.760
And if you look back
over the last five years

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:07.260
since he made that
statement, Google

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:10.950
has continued to invest in
optimizing and modernizing

00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.350
and bringing in the
technologies and tools that

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.780
allow you to bring your
enterprise Microsoft

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:18.800
workloads into Google Cloud.

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:20.540
Today, thousands
of our customers

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:23.780
are running Windows
workloads in Google Cloud,

00:01:23.780 --> 00:01:27.080
amounting to billions of core
hours every year of Windows

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:28.690
workloads.

00:01:28.690 --> 00:01:32.200
Some of our recent customers,
including Travix and Volusion

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:34.660
and, most recently,
TELUS International,

00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:37.180
talk about how they have
brought their Windows

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:41.820
workloads into Google and what
a great decision it's been.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.220
So why do people want to
bring Microsoft workloads

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:45.120
into Google?

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:49.260
First and foremost, Google
has phenomenal infrastructure.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:51.780
Between our underlying
compute capabilities,

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.210
the speed of our storage, the
robustness of our network,

00:01:54.210 --> 00:01:57.390
and the security that
we bake into everything,

00:01:57.390 --> 00:02:00.030
Google always wants to make
sure that we provide the best

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:03.430
for our customers that we can.

00:02:03.430 --> 00:02:07.270
Google also preferences
giving customers choice.

00:02:07.270 --> 00:02:10.030
We want to be able to
provide an array of offerings

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:12.340
so that customers
can choose which

00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:16.470
different pieces are going
to fit their solutions best.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:20.220
And Google, first and foremost,
is committed to modernization

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:22.450
and to an open platform.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:24.830
We are devoted to the
open source community,

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:31.290
and we are the innovators
behind things like Kubernetes,

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:35.280
TensorFlow, GKE, and other tools
that are commonly used today

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:36.855
throughout a variety of clouds.

00:02:39.590 --> 00:02:42.250
So let's talk a little bit
about our three staged approach.

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.060
First, is to migrate.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:45.610
So we help you with
a lift and shift,

00:02:45.610 --> 00:02:47.590
moving from your
on-premises data center

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:50.770
into Google Cloud, migrating
your VMs into our Google

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:53.080
Compute Engine, and
potentially even

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:55.360
upgrading those versions
of the operating system

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:56.580
where possible.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.360
I'll talk a little bit more
about that in a moment.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:02.440
This is using our Migrate
for Compute Engine tool.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:04.900
By doing this, we work with
you to get the right balance

00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:07.630
of your bring your own
licensing capabilities versus

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:09.820
a pay-as-you-go licensing.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:12.880
We help you take advantage
of Google technology,

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:16.480
such as CPU overcommit
and custom VM shapes,

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.030
and we allow you to leverage
suspending and resuming

00:03:19.030 --> 00:03:22.000
your VMs, so that you
can save on costs when

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:24.622
things aren't actually needed.

00:03:24.622 --> 00:03:26.830
Next, we'll talk a little
bit about our modernization

00:03:26.830 --> 00:03:27.753
opportunities.

00:03:27.753 --> 00:03:29.170
These are the ways
in which we can

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:32.320
help you bring your Windows
applications into containers

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:34.360
and still be in Windows.

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:37.120
Or taking advantage of our
fully managed service offerings,

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:40.960
like Cloud SQL or
Managed Active Directory.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:44.170
And all of this results in a
greater efficiency, tighter bin

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:48.220
packing, and better
cost optimization.

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:50.560
Lastly, we can talk
about how we can help you

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:52.660
transform your applications.

00:03:52.660 --> 00:03:57.280
Moving to more open standards,
like Postgres or MySQL,

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.000
or moving your .NET on
Windows into .NET core.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.820
Or lastly taking your .NET
applications and containerizing

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:07.840
them for ease of management
and ease of scale.

00:04:07.840 --> 00:04:09.640
Where do I go?

00:04:09.640 --> 00:04:11.500
As I said, Google
wants to provide you

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:13.840
a variety of choices
so that you can choose

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:18.640
per workload what the best
destination is for your Windows

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:20.190
applications.

00:04:20.190 --> 00:04:21.940
First choice is the
Google Compute Engine.

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:25.580
This is our standard
Google VM environment

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:29.350
where we can help you bring
your on-prem VMs using

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:31.810
our Migrate for Compute Engine.

00:04:31.810 --> 00:04:34.060
If there are applications
that it makes more sense

00:04:34.060 --> 00:04:36.490
to stay in VMware,
we can help you

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:40.726
stay in VMware with our
VMware as a service offering.

00:04:40.726 --> 00:04:44.570
It allows you to leverage
native VMware tools like HCX

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:48.200
to do your migrations
and helps you

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.860
move those workloads
from VMware on premises

00:04:51.860 --> 00:04:54.710
into VMware and Google
Cloud, where you can still

00:04:54.710 --> 00:04:57.050
take advantage of all of
the Google Cloud features

00:04:57.050 --> 00:04:59.560
and services.

00:04:59.560 --> 00:05:02.820
Lastly, for those items that
are appropriate to containerize,

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:06.480
we can help you with our
Migrate for Anthos tool to take

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:10.200
your .NET applications that
are running in VMs today

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:13.650
and actually put them into
Windows containers running

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:16.370
in Kubernetes.

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:18.710
Similarly, for
Microsoft SQL, you

00:05:18.710 --> 00:05:21.350
have the opportunity to continue
running your own Microsoft

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:25.340
SQL on Windows in our
Google Compute Engine

00:05:25.340 --> 00:05:27.460
in a virtual machine.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:31.030
Or we can help you
migrate to SQL for Linux,

00:05:31.030 --> 00:05:34.390
thus eliminating the need to
pay for the underlying operating

00:05:34.390 --> 00:05:38.650
system of Windows
because Linux is free.

00:05:38.650 --> 00:05:41.410
Or, if you want to get out
of the business of managing

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:43.330
your own SQL server,
we can help you

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:46.660
move to cloud SQL, where we
were the fully managed service

00:05:46.660 --> 00:05:49.760
offering, and you
just need to tap in.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:52.330
So a little bit of an
overview on licensing

00:05:52.330 --> 00:05:54.260
with a quick disclaimer.

00:05:54.260 --> 00:05:57.530
This is guidance at best.

00:05:57.530 --> 00:05:59.500
Please make sure that
you check the details

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:03.880
of your individual
Microsoft licensing.

00:06:03.880 --> 00:06:05.560
So essentially,
there are two models

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:08.110
for how you can take care
of your Microsoft licenses

00:06:08.110 --> 00:06:09.910
within Google Cloud.

00:06:09.910 --> 00:06:12.320
The first is very easy
and straightforward.

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:13.870
It's just pay as you go.

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:16.030
If you'd like to deploy
a Microsoft Windows

00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:19.840
Server in Google Cloud, when
you're creating that VM,

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:23.320
you select the
Windows Server image.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:26.020
And that image will not
only have the Windows Server

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:30.970
operating system, it will also
have the necessary licensure.

00:06:30.970 --> 00:06:33.400
And that gets billed
on per second basis.

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:35.320
You spin it up,
the billing starts.

00:06:35.320 --> 00:06:38.380
You spin it down,
the billing stops.

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:40.480
Similarly, for SQL
server, if you'd

00:06:40.480 --> 00:06:42.250
like to select one
of our Windows images

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:45.280
that has SQL server
already installed,

00:06:45.280 --> 00:06:47.220
we can charge you per
minute for the time

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:49.600
that that SQL server is up.

00:06:49.600 --> 00:06:53.470
And then when that SQL server
is down, the billing stops.

00:06:53.470 --> 00:06:57.060
This is particularly useful
for test or dev or staging

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:59.790
environments that
don't need to run 24/7

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:02.940
that you want to be able
to bring up and bring down.

00:07:02.940 --> 00:07:05.820
Similarly, for other Microsoft
applications like SharePoint

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:09.750
or Exchange, we leverage our
third party partners that are

00:07:09.750 --> 00:07:12.150
able to provide images and
pay-as-you-go licensing

00:07:12.150 --> 00:07:13.110
and cost.

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:17.870
For our BYO licenses, we offer
our sole tenant nodes, which

00:07:17.870 --> 00:07:20.750
we'll discuss in a moment.

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:23.000
Your eligibility to
bring your own license

00:07:23.000 --> 00:07:26.100
will still depend on
your Microsoft licensure.

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:30.110
But where you are entitled
to bring your licenses,

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:32.570
our sole tenant nodes
allow you the place

00:07:32.570 --> 00:07:35.510
to do that with
dedicated hardware.

00:07:35.510 --> 00:07:38.510
SQL server by the terms of
your software assurance.

00:07:38.510 --> 00:07:41.810
You have the ability to bring
your SQL server licenses

00:07:41.810 --> 00:07:45.700
wherever you'd like, as well
as SharePoint and exchange

00:07:45.700 --> 00:07:48.460
in other Microsoft applications.

00:07:48.460 --> 00:07:51.368
This is the use case
for 24x7 applications,

00:07:51.368 --> 00:07:53.410
where you need to keep
them running all the time.

00:07:57.070 --> 00:07:59.970
So some people have
discussed October 2019 when

00:07:59.970 --> 00:08:01.980
it comes to Microsoft licenses.

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:06.060
There was a change,
based on October 2019,

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:10.830
for licenses purchased after
that versus licenses purchased

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:12.260
before that.

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:15.540
For Windows Server
licenses purchased

00:08:15.540 --> 00:08:19.950
before October of 2019,
as long as these licenses

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:21.750
were purchased prior to that--

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:24.870
or associated with
larger agreements that

00:08:24.870 --> 00:08:26.850
were created prior to that--

00:08:26.850 --> 00:08:29.700
you should be able to bring
those windows licenses

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:33.870
to GCP within the guardrails--
within the boundaries--

00:08:33.870 --> 00:08:35.929
of your Microsoft license.

00:08:35.929 --> 00:08:39.335
For SQL server, as long as
you have software assurance,

00:08:39.335 --> 00:08:41.960
you're able to continue to bring
those licenses over to Google.

00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:48.080
In order to make sure that
we have all of our Is dotted

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:50.900
and Ts crossed, we've engaged
the services of a third party

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:53.270
company called SoftwareOne.

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:57.710
SoftwareOne is a premier partner
of both Google and Microsoft

00:08:57.710 --> 00:09:00.440
and has provided us
prescriptive guidance

00:09:00.440 --> 00:09:03.930
on the details of
Microsoft licensure.

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:07.080
And as you can see in both
cases, Windows Server and SQL

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:09.270
server, there is
likely a path to be

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:12.800
able to bring those
licenses over.

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:15.050
One of the requirements
for being in compliance

00:09:15.050 --> 00:09:16.850
with Microsoft
licensing is the ability

00:09:16.850 --> 00:09:19.910
to generate a report
of the operating

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:22.430
system and the servers
that they're running on.

00:09:22.430 --> 00:09:24.500
Google takes that
complexity away

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:26.610
by providing these
reports for you on demand.

00:09:29.150 --> 00:09:31.760
As I mentioned,
sole-tenant nodes.

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:34.460
Sole-tenant nodes
mean that you have

00:09:34.460 --> 00:09:37.640
sole control over the
underlying hardware

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:40.760
for the virtual machines
that you're deploying.

00:09:40.760 --> 00:09:44.780
You can mix and match
Linux and Windows VMs

00:09:44.780 --> 00:09:46.830
on those sole-tenant nodes.

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:49.550
But, in addition,
you can flip a switch

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:52.670
that allows you to
trigger a CPU overcommit.

00:09:52.670 --> 00:09:54.950
Essentially, what
that means is you

00:09:54.950 --> 00:09:58.340
make it appear as though
there's twice as many CPUs

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:00.350
available on that host.

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:03.020
And for those applications
that are not overly resource

00:10:03.020 --> 00:10:06.860
intensive, it allows you
to pack twice as many VMs

00:10:06.860 --> 00:10:08.630
onto that same hardware.

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:11.300
So you can get much
more cost-effective use

00:10:11.300 --> 00:10:16.330
of that underlying hardware
that you're now using.

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:20.430
Custom VMs are an outstanding
tool for SQL server.

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:24.810
So SQL server is
licensed by CPU,

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:27.600
but is very, very RAM hungry.

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:31.170
For many Cloud providers,
what that means is, in order

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:34.260
to get the additional
memory that SQL wants,

00:10:34.260 --> 00:10:37.590
you have to increase
your VCPU count.

00:10:37.590 --> 00:10:41.100
Meaning you're not only being
charged for the CPU cores,

00:10:41.100 --> 00:10:44.910
but you're also having to pay
licensure for SQL and Windows

00:10:44.910 --> 00:10:49.050
for those additional use
that you really don't need.

00:10:49.050 --> 00:10:51.150
With custom VMs
and GCP, we allow

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:54.960
you to scale that RAM and CPU
core separately so that you

00:10:54.960 --> 00:10:56.700
can create that
custom shape that's

00:10:56.700 --> 00:10:59.520
going to give you the optimal
amount of RAM for your SQL

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:02.340
Server or other
applications while still not

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:06.750
having to overpay for CPUs
that you don't actually need.

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:09.480
Windows Server 2008
is another topic

00:11:09.480 --> 00:11:12.120
that we frequently hear
from our customers.

00:11:12.120 --> 00:11:14.970
Windows Server 2008
R2 has gone end

00:11:14.970 --> 00:11:17.640
of life, which
means that Microsoft

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:21.810
will no longer provide security
updates or patches for it

00:11:21.810 --> 00:11:25.260
without having to purchase
potentially expensive

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:28.880
extended security updates.

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:31.040
Migrate for Compute
Engine allows

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:35.810
you to take those 2008 R2
servers from your data center

00:11:35.810 --> 00:11:37.820
and, in the process
of migration,

00:11:37.820 --> 00:11:42.950
actually upgrade them in flight,
so that when they land in GCE,

00:11:42.950 --> 00:11:47.180
they land as 2012 servers, which
is a fully supported operating

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:48.440
system.

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:51.710
That is provided at no
cost to our customers.

00:11:51.710 --> 00:11:54.050
By using the different
tools and technology

00:11:54.050 --> 00:11:58.520
that Google provides, optimizing
your per second billing,

00:11:58.520 --> 00:12:01.040
being able to shape
the custom VMs,

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:04.520
taking advantage of preemptible
VMs, so for those workloads

00:12:04.520 --> 00:12:07.760
that just need episodic
access to compute,

00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:09.590
you can spin them up
and spin them down

00:12:09.590 --> 00:12:12.580
at a greatly reduced price.

00:12:12.580 --> 00:12:15.700
We also have an ongoing
recommendation system

00:12:15.700 --> 00:12:19.270
where you can see
at a VM by VM level

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:22.960
what the recommendations are
for scaling up or scaling down

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:26.140
based on your actual
utilization of those VMs,

00:12:26.140 --> 00:12:28.960
allowing you to right-size
your spend and right-size

00:12:28.960 --> 00:12:31.130
your utilization of resources.

00:12:31.130 --> 00:12:33.940
Let's talk a little bit
now about modernizing.

00:12:33.940 --> 00:12:38.080
So some of the ways that we
help you modernize around SQL.

00:12:38.080 --> 00:12:40.900
By moving to SQL on
Linux, for example,

00:12:40.900 --> 00:12:43.630
you remove the underlying
need to license the Windows

00:12:43.630 --> 00:12:45.580
operating system.

00:12:45.580 --> 00:12:47.770
By moving to Managed
SQL, you don't

00:12:47.770 --> 00:12:50.110
have to pay the SQL
license costs at all

00:12:50.110 --> 00:12:53.810
because all of that is managed
within our cloud SQL offering.

00:12:53.810 --> 00:12:57.650
And by moving that
responsibility of management,

00:12:57.650 --> 00:12:59.980
we may even be able to help
you move from enterprise

00:12:59.980 --> 00:13:02.200
to standard to web.

00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:05.620
And, as you can see, moving
from server enterprise to SQL

00:13:05.620 --> 00:13:09.430
server standard can be a
tremendous cost savings.

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:12.190
Being able to move all
the way to SQL server web

00:13:12.190 --> 00:13:13.825
can be a phenomenal
cost savings.

00:13:17.840 --> 00:13:20.570
Windows Server
containers in GKE.

00:13:20.570 --> 00:13:24.170
This is our ability to
support Windows containers--

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:26.810
that can either be
spun up from scratch

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:30.590
or can come over as a result of
our Migrate for Anthos tool--

00:13:30.590 --> 00:13:33.080
that can pick up your
VMs from your data center

00:13:33.080 --> 00:13:36.110
and deliver them as
containers in Kubernetes.

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:41.030
One of the best advantages
of containerizing

00:13:41.030 --> 00:13:44.040
these applications
is license cost.

00:13:44.040 --> 00:13:48.690
With your Microsoft licenses,
once the underlying VM host

00:13:48.690 --> 00:13:51.460
has been licensed for
the operating system,

00:13:51.460 --> 00:13:53.160
you can deploy as
many containers

00:13:53.160 --> 00:13:57.580
on top of that operating system
as the hardware will support.

00:13:57.580 --> 00:14:00.090
So, again, this provides
much better bin packing,

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:02.790
much greater efficiency,
much more cost

00:14:02.790 --> 00:14:07.418
effective use of the resources
you're already paying for.

00:14:07.418 --> 00:14:09.460
Our managed service offering
for Active Directory

00:14:09.460 --> 00:14:11.860
allows you to get out from
under the burden of having

00:14:11.860 --> 00:14:14.920
to manage your Active
Directory infrastructure

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:18.760
and still be able to use it as
the security and profiling tool

00:14:18.760 --> 00:14:21.010
that you want to be
able to use it for.

00:14:21.010 --> 00:14:23.950
Typically, what we see when
we sit down and we work with

00:14:23.950 --> 00:14:27.730
customers on optimizing their
costs once they've moved

00:14:27.730 --> 00:14:32.380
their Microsoft workloads
into GCP is we can see as much

00:14:32.380 --> 00:14:36.940
as a 50% cost savings by
blending your BYO licenses

00:14:36.940 --> 00:14:40.690
and your pay-as-you-go licenses,
by being able to take advantage

00:14:40.690 --> 00:14:44.080
of the CPU overcommit, and
using some of the other tools

00:14:44.080 --> 00:14:48.050
and technology that
Google provides,

00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:51.080
which allows you to then take
that savings and reinvest it

00:14:51.080 --> 00:14:52.710
in your transformation.

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:54.650
This is where you
really want to end up

00:14:54.650 --> 00:14:56.370
when all is said and done.

00:14:56.370 --> 00:14:57.890
This is moving
into Cloud Native.

00:14:57.890 --> 00:15:00.150
This is moving into open source.

00:15:00.150 --> 00:15:03.320
This is what gives you the
scalability and the flexibility

00:15:03.320 --> 00:15:06.470
that really underpin your
digital transformation.

00:15:06.470 --> 00:15:10.850
So being able to move from SQL
Server to PostGres or MySQL,

00:15:10.850 --> 00:15:13.940
or helping you migrate your .NET
for Windows applications onto

00:15:13.940 --> 00:15:18.180
.NET Core that runs on Linux,
or even just containerizing

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:22.010
your .NET applications, so you
can start making incremental

00:15:22.010 --> 00:15:24.970
steps towards containerization.

00:15:24.970 --> 00:15:28.480
Using our Google Kubernetes
engine or, possibly,

00:15:28.480 --> 00:15:30.250
even going into Anthos.

00:15:30.250 --> 00:15:33.310
Ultimately, we want to be
able to partner with you.

00:15:33.310 --> 00:15:36.070
To look at your Microsoft
workloads and help you identify

00:15:36.070 --> 00:15:39.080
which applications
should be retired,

00:15:39.080 --> 00:15:41.570
which ones can be
migrated into GCP,

00:15:41.570 --> 00:15:44.920
such as third party
ISV applications,

00:15:44.920 --> 00:15:49.170
which internally created
applications are easy rewrites,

00:15:49.170 --> 00:15:53.330
such as moving from
.NET to .NET Core,

00:15:53.330 --> 00:15:56.450
or which ones are going to be
more complicated or that may

00:15:56.450 --> 00:16:00.140
yield better results by moving
into things like serverless,

00:16:00.140 --> 00:16:03.207
through App Engine and Cloud
Run, and then, ultimately,

00:16:03.207 --> 00:16:05.540
what are the applications
that you're just going to have

00:16:05.540 --> 00:16:09.230
to maintain until their
functionality is completely

00:16:09.230 --> 00:16:11.010
replaced with
other applications.

00:16:11.010 --> 00:16:13.160
So, in summary, we
want to help you

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:16.130
start at the left with
your Microsoft workloads

00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:17.930
and your Microsoft
servers running

00:16:17.930 --> 00:16:21.680
in your on-premises location,
lift and shift and migrate them

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:25.610
into Google Cloud using
our tools and technologies,

00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:28.700
working with you to optimize and
get the right balance of spend

00:16:28.700 --> 00:16:31.490
for your compute and
for your licensure,

00:16:31.490 --> 00:16:33.620
and then, ultimately,
help you through

00:16:33.620 --> 00:16:37.507
your digital transformation into
Cloud Native and open source.

00:16:37.507 --> 00:16:39.590
Thank you, and we look
forward to working with you

00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:41.290
